From: EglantinePS5 <Vampinity@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] waveydude: pipeline builds take turns while memory is short (EDEN_PIPELINE_MEMORY_GATE_MIB)

DRAFT, not built, not run on a console.

With EDEN_PIPELINE_MEMORY_GATE_MIB=<n>, a graphics or compute pipeline build that starts while the
pool's largest free block is under n MiB waits until no other build is inside the gate, the pool is
over the mark again, or EDEN_PIPELINE_MEMORY_GATE_MS (default 2000) has passed; then it goes ahead,
never refused. Over the mark nothing changes. Off unless set: with it unset no build waits, nothing
is counted and nothing is logged. The free block comes from the front end (eden_pool_largest_free,
headless/performance.cpp, cached 20 ms; draft-0554-front.diff); without it the gate never waits.

From waveydude/Eglantine-PS5- (his tree = our T85 plus his commits), commit b9f52d98e8 by
waveydude, his inner patch 0915-ocarina-pipeline-memory-gate.patch and his edit of
headless/performance.cpp. pipeline_memory_gate.h is his file unchanged up to his namespace's end;
the switch names, the default limit and the waiting rule are his.

Changed for our tree, and why:
- The gate's settings, counters and the pool query are functions in
  Vulkan::PipelineMemoryGate (declared at the end of his header, defined in vk_pipeline_cache.cpp)
  instead of his file-local ones, so the pipeline constructors can share them (below).
- His report line came every 32 more waits; here also at most one per 10 s, and a log line that
  finds no memory is skipped (inside these functions a std::bad_alloc from the logger would drop
  the pipeline being built).
- Ours, not his: on the GPU thread (a build with build_in_parallel that is not a translator) the
  gate's free memory reads 0 ("unknown": go ahead) while a builder's heap request waits for memory
  (eden_heap_wanted(), EDEN_UP_HEAP_WAIT_MS). That wait counts on the texture collector, which runs
  on the GPU thread; parked at the gate behind the same builder, the two would wait on each other
  until the gate's limit. Without EDEN_UP_HEAP_WAIT_MS nothing changes.
- Ours, not his, own switch: EDEN_PIPELINE_MEMORY_GATE_WORKERS=1 (exact 1, only with the mark).
  In this tree a pipeline made during play is translated inside the gate on the GPU thread, but its
  driver compile (the large shader compiler allocations, and 0552's one retry) runs later as a job
  on a VkPipelineBuilder thread, outside it: the gate alone sees only the threads that translate
  (the GPU thread, EDEN_UP_ASYNC_TRANSLATION's translators, and the builders of the disk cache,
  the shader preparation and the shader set, which build whole). With _WORKERS that job takes its
  turn too, 0552's retry inside the same turn. A build that runs on the calling thread (no worker)
  is inside that thread's gate already and is not gated twice.

Left out: his host test tools/test-pipeline-memory-gate.cpp (frontend repo, not needed to build).
His placement between SurviveDriverOutOfMemory's comment and its function was not kept.

Anchors of tools/prepare-vulkan-port.py counted per file before and after (vk_pipeline_cache.cpp
18, vk_graphics_pipeline.cpp 11, vk_compute_pipeline.cpp 2, including the _cost.cpp generator's
expressions): unchanged.

---

From the review, on top of the draft: with _WORKERS the GPU thread's own builds never wait at
the gate (it draws the frame and runs the texture collector the builders may be waiting on: a
burst of new pipelines could otherwise hold it up to EDEN_PIPELINE_MEMORY_GATE_MS each, with no
limit per frame); the worker gates write the report line too, and it shows the builds inside and
the free memory; the wait limit is capped at 10 s.

